Host-side gtest unit tests for crosspoint-reader.

Build and run:

  cmake -S test -B build/test
  cmake --build build/test
  ctest --test-dir build/test --output-on-failure -j

Run a single suite directly:

  cmake --build build/test --target StreamingJsonParserTest
  build/test/streaming_json_parser/StreamingJsonParserTest --gtest_filter='*'

Google Test is fetched via CMake FetchContent on first configure; the pinned
version lives in test/CMakeLists.txt.

PlatformIO firmware builds (`pio run -e default`, `pio run -e gh_release`) do
not run host tests. Run `pio run -t unit-tests` or the CMake/CTest commands
above separately. CI runs firmware builds and host tests in distinct jobs on
pull requests and pushes to `master` or `crossfront`.

CrossFront host coverage (`CrossFrontSafetyTest`) checks hardware-ID formatting,
token shape/termination, rejects unsafe file assignment IDs, names, and SD
folders, and verifies backup/rollback on failed file replacement. ESP random/MAC
and SD operations are stubbed; these tests cannot verify hardware entropy,
HTTP/TLS, SD behavior, or display rendering.

Before flashing a custom build after an upstream release merge, use a spare SD
card and verify manually:

  - Pairing and token rotation keep the same 12-character device ID across
    setup QR, config, sleep image, and file-sync requests.
  - Wi-Fi failure and timeout leave the previous cached sleep image intact;
    HTTP 304 preserves BMP/ETag without rewriting the BMP.
  - A 200 response updates BMP/ETag; invalid BMP, 4xx, and 5xx paths show a
    safe fallback rather than a broken or stale screen.
  - File sync with a valid book/font/sleep file writes only to its intended
    folder; cancellation, SD-full, interrupted transfer, and rename failure
    leave no corrupt final file and do not acknowledge the assignment.
  - Check free heap, largest allocatable block, boot/wake logs, and flash target
    on X3/X4 before trusting the firmware on a daily-use device.
